%[text] %[text:anchor:T_8917F6D5] # Matrix Operations
%[text] Wherever you find digital data, you will find matrices.
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_H_9D82661E]  ![Rhino with pixel values](text:image:3927)
%[text]{"align":"center"}  *Grayscale images are large matrices of pixel intensities*
%[text] It's easy to see how you can apply a mathematical operation to a single element of a matrix:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $58 \\times 3 = 174${"altText":"58 times 3 = 174"}.
%[text] However, it's not as clear how these operators apply to full matrices.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\left( \\matrix{ 58 & 149 \\cr 114 & 134 } \\right) \\times \\left( \\matrix{ 1 & 0 \\cr -2 & 3 } \\right) = ?${"altText":"( matrix \[ 58 , 149 ; 114 , 134 \] ) times ( matrix \[ 1 , 0 ; -2 , 3 \] ) = ?"}
%[text] In this live script, you'll learn the definitions of basic matrix operations and practice computing them by hand and in MATLAB.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] **Before you get started:**
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_H_BF646A47] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**.  Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![live script code hidden icon](text:image:7eb9) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:5045) Although the code is hidden, some interactivity requires familiarity with MATLAB. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2 hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_H_8F8A032D] ![Warning symbol](text:image:8a3d)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one. Some sections depend on variables created in prior sections and will generate errors if run out of order.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4D969F44] ## Matrix operations
%[text] - In this live script, the same numeric values are used in $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"}, $\\mathbf{B}${"altText":"B"}, $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"}, and $c${"altText":"c"}.
%[text] - You can run this section to generate new random versions of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"}, $\\mathbf{B}${"altText":"B"}, $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"}, and $c${"altText":"c"}. \
  %[control:button:25c8]{"position":[1,2]}
[Ai,Bi,ci,vi] = DefineMatrices;
disp("Matrices have been (re)defined.")
%[text] Note that the matrices defined here are $10 \\times 10${"altText":"10 times 10"}, but you can adjust the sizes in the following sections to select a suitably sized submatrix.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_353623A8] ### Definition
%[text] A matrix is a rectangular set of elements. The $m \\times n${"altText":"m times n"} matrix shown here has $m${"altText":"m"} rows and $n${"altText":"n"} columns.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{A} = \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\na\_{11}  &  a\_{12} & \\ldots  & a\_{1n} \\\\\na\_{21} & a\_{22}   & \\ldots  & a\_{2n} \\\\\n\\vdots &  \\vdots   & \\ddots  & \\vdots \\\\\na\_{m1}  & a\_{m2} & \\ldots  & a\_{mn} \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A = matrix \[ a\_(11) , a\_(12) , ... , a\_(1n) ; a\_(21) , a\_(22) , ... , a\_(2n) ; ... , ... , diagonal continuation , ... ; a\_(m1) , a\_(m2) , ... , a\_(mn) \]"}
%[text] Each element is denoted $a\_{i,j}${"altText":"a\_(i,j)"}, where $i${"altText":"i"} refers to the row and $j${"altText":"j"} to the column.
%[text] ![](text:image:8df3) **Try**. 
%[text] - Adjust the number of rows ($m${"altText":"m"}) and columns ($n${"altText":"n"}) to view $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} with different sizes.
%[text] - View a concrete example of a numeric matrix by selecting the checkbox. \
m = 3; %[control:slider:2e88]{"position":[5,6]}
n = 6;  %[control:slider:3cd0]{"position":[5,6]}
numeric = true; %[control:checkbox:1dde]{"position":[11,15]}
  %[control:button:4447]{"position":[1,2]}
ValidateMatrices % This helper ensures that the matrices are defined
if(numeric) 
    A = sym(Ai(1:m,1:n))
else
    A = sym('a',[m,n])
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F5696E6A] ### Addition and subtraction
%[text] To add two $n \\times m${"altText":"n times m"} matrices, add their corresponding elements.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{B} = \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\na\_{11}  &  a\_{12} & \\ldots  & a\_{1n} \\\\\na\_{21} & a\_{22}   & \\ldots  & a\_{2n} \\\\\n\\vdots &  \\vdots   & \\ddots  & \\vdots \\\\\na\_{m1}  & a\_{m2} & \\ldots  & a\_{mn} \n\\end{array}\\right) \n+ \n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\nb\_{11}  &  b\_{12} & \\ldots  & b\_{1n} \\\\\nb\_{21} & b\_{22}   & \\ldots  & b\_{2n} \\\\\n\\vdots &  \\vdots   & \\ddots  & \\vdots \\\\\nb\_{m1}  & b\_{m2} & \\ldots  & b\_{mn} \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"A + B = matrix \[ a\_(11) , a\_(12) , ... , a\_(1n) ; a\_(21) , a\_(22) , ... , a\_(2n) ; ... , ... , diagonal continuation , ... ; a\_(m1) , a\_(m2) , ... , a\_(mn) \] + matrix \[ b\_(11) , b\_(12) , ... , b\_(1n) ; b\_(21) , b\_(22) , ... , b\_(2n) ; ... , ... , diagonal continuation , ... ; b\_(m1) , b\_(m2) , ... , b\_(mn) \]"}
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $= \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\na\_{11} + b\_{11}  &  a\_{12} + b\_{12} & \\ldots  & a\_{1n} +b\_{1n}  \\\\\na\_{21} + b\_{21} & a\_{22} + b\_{22} & \\ldots  & a\_{2n} + b\_{2n} \\\\\n\\vdots &  \\vdots   & \\ddots  & \\vdots \\\\\na\_{m1}  + b\_{m1} & a\_{m2} + b\_{m2} & \\ldots  & a\_{mn}  + b\_{mn} \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"= matrix \[ a\_(11) + b\_(11) , a\_(12) + b\_(12) , ... , a\_(1n) +b\_(1n) ; a\_(21) + b\_(21) , a\_(22) + b\_(22) , ... , a\_(2n) + b\_(2n) ; ... , ... , diagonal continuation , ... ; a\_(m1) + b\_(m1) , a\_(m2) + b\_(m2) , ... , a\_(mn) + b\_(mn) \]"} 
%[text] To subtract matrices, subtract the corresponding elements. To add or subtract two matrices, they must be the same size. 
%[text] ![](text:image:6b4b) **Exercise**. Subtract matrices $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} and $\\mathbf{B}${"altText":"B"}. You can adjust their sizes. 
m = 2; %[control:slider:9d8f]{"position":[5,6]}
n = 4;  %[control:slider:3c63]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:6ad2]{"position":[1,2]}
ValidateMatrices % This helper ensures that the matrices are defined
A = sym(Ai(1:m,1:n));
B = sym(Bi(1:m,1:n));
displayFormula(["'A = '", "A", "'          B = '", "B"])
ShowSolution = true; %[control:checkbox:65e3]{"position":[16,20]}
if(ShowSolution)
    AminusB = A-B;
    displayFormula(["'A - B = '", "AminusB"])
end
%[text] ![](text:image:46b1) **Reflect.** Consider the matrix $\\mathbf{D}${"altText":"D"} shown below. Can you compute $\\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{D}${"altText":"A + D"}?
BD = sym(Bi(1:m,1:(n+1)));
displayFormula(["'D = '", "BD"])
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C7649BF6] ### Multiplication by a scalar
%[text] When multiplying a matrix $A${"altText":"A"} by a scalar $c${"altText":"c"}, multiply each element by the scalar.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $c\\mathbf{A} = \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\nca\_{11}  &  ca\_{12} & \\ldots  & ca\_{1n} \\\\\nca\_{21} & ca\_{22}   & \\ldots  & ca\_{2n} \\\\\n\\vdots &  \\vdots   & \\ddots  & \\vdots \\\\\nca\_{m1}  & ca\_{m2} & \\ldots  & ca\_{mn} \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"cA = matrix \[ ca\_(11) , ca\_(12) , ... , ca\_(1n) ; ca\_(21) , ca\_(22) , ... , ca\_(2n) ; ... , ... , diagonal continuation , ... ; ca\_(m1) , ca\_(m2) , ... , ca\_(mn) \]"}
%[text] ![](text:image:2a85) **Exercise**. Multiply the matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} by the scalar $c${"altText":"c"}. You can adjust the size of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"}.
m = 2; %[control:slider:66ef]{"position":[5,6]}
n = 2;  %[control:slider:0240]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:0b71]{"position":[1,2]}
ValidateMatrices % This helper ensures that the matrices are defined
A = sym(Ai(1:m,1:n));
displayFormula(["'A = '", "A"])
c = sym(ci);
displayFormula(["'c = '", "c"])
ShowSolution2 = true; %[control:checkbox:4f7e]{"position":[17,21]}
if(ShowSolution2)
    cA = ci*A;
    displayFormula(["'cA = '", "cA"])
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3F08260A] ### Multiplication by a column vector
%[text] The product of a $m \\times n${"altText":"m times n"} matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} and an $n${"altText":"n"} element column vector $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"} yields a column vector. The entries of the product $\\mathbf{Av}${"altText":"Av"} are the dot product of the rows of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} with the vector $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"}:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{Av} = \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\na\_{11}  &  a\_{12} & \\ldots  & a\_{1n} \\\\\na\_{21} & a\_{22}   & \\ldots  & a\_{2n} \\\\\n\\vdots &  \\vdots   & \\ddots  & \\vdots \\\\\na\_{m1}  & a\_{m2} & \\ldots  & a\_{mn} \n\\end{array}\\right)\n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\nv\_{1} \\\\\nv\_{2} \\\\\n\\vdots \\\\\nv\_{n} \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"Av = matrix \[ a\_(11) , a\_(12) , ... , a\_(1n) ; a\_(21) , a\_(22) , ... , a\_(2n) ; ... , ... , diagonal continuation , ... ; a\_(m1) , a\_(m2) , ... , a\_(mn) \] matrix \[ v\_(1) ; v\_(2) ; ... ; v\_(n) \]"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $= \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\na\_{11} v\_1  +  a\_{12} v\_2  + \\ldots  + a\_{1n}v\_n \\\\\na\_{21} v\_1  +  a\_{22} v\_2  + \\ldots  + a\_{2n}v\_n\\\\\n\\vdots \\\\\na\_{m1} v\_1  +  a\_{m2} v\_2  + \\ldots  + a\_{mn}v\_n\\\\\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"= matrix \[ a\_(11) v\_1 + a\_(12) v\_2 + ... + a\_(1n)v\_n ; a\_(21) v\_1 + a\_(22) v\_2 + ... + a\_(2n)v\_n; ... ; a\_(m1) v\_1 + a\_(m2) v\_2 + ... + a\_(mn)v\_n; \]"}
%[text] To take the dot product of the rows of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} and the vector $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"}, the number of columns in $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} must match the number of elements in $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"}. This implies $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"v"} must have $n${"altText":"n"} entries.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Matrix-vector multiplication](text:image:01d9)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Animation of matrix-vector multiplication. Each entry in the result is created by taking the dot product of the matrix row and the vector.*
%[text] ![](text:image:63b0) **Exercise**. Compute the matrix-vector product $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}${"altText":"A v"}. You can adjust the sizes.
m = 3; %[control:slider:88bc]{"position":[5,6]}
n = 2;  %[control:slider:5850]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:61d9]{"position":[1,2]}
ValidateMatrices % This helper ensures that the matrices are defined
A = sym(Ai(1:m,1:n));
displayFormula(["'A = '", "A"])
v = sym(vi(1:n));
displayFormula(["'v = '", "v"])
ValidateMatrices % This helper ensures that the matrices are defined
ShowSolution3 = true; %[control:checkbox:9af2]{"position":[17,21]}
if(ShowSolution3)
    Av = A*v;
    displayFormula(["'Av = '", "Av"])
end
%[text] ![](text:image:4cd8) **Reflect.** Consider the vector $\\mathbf{u}${"altText":"u"} shown below. Can you compute $\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{u}${"altText":"Au"}?
u = sym(vi(1:(n+2)));
displayFormula(["'u = '", "u"])
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BE0949F7] ### Multiplication by another matrix
%[text] The product of a $m \\times n${"altText":"m times n"} matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} and an $n \\times p${"altText":"n times p"} matrix $\\mathbf{B}\n${"altText":"B"} is a $m \\times p${"altText":"m times p"} matrix $\\mathbf{C}\n${"altText":"C"}, defined below.  Note that the "inner" dimensions of $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} and $\\mathbf{B}\n${"altText":"B"} must match for their product to be defined.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{AB} = \\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\na\_{11}  &  a\_{12} & \\ldots  & a\_{1n} \\\\\na\_{21} & a\_{22}   & \\ldots  & a\_{2n} \\\\\n\\vdots &  \\vdots   & \\ddots  & \\vdots \\\\\na\_{m1}  & a\_{m2} & \\ldots  & a\_{mn} \n\\end{array}\\right)\n\n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\nb\_{11}  & b\_{12} & \\ldots  & b\_{1p} \\\\\nb\_{21} & b\_{22}   & \\ldots  & b\_{2p} \\\\\n\\vdots &  \\vdots   & \\ddots  & \\vdots \\\\\nb\_{n1}  & b\_{n2} & \\ldots  & b\_{np} \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"AB = matrix \[ a\_(11) , a\_(12) , ... , a\_(1n) ; a\_(21) , a\_(22) , ... , a\_(2n) ; ... , ... , diagonal continuation , ... ; a\_(m1) , a\_(m2) , ... , a\_(mn) \] matrix \[ b\_(11) , b\_(12) , ... , b\_(1p) ; b\_(21) , b\_(22) , ... , b\_(2p) ; ... , ... , diagonal continuation , ... ; b\_(n1) , b\_(n2) , ... , b\_(np) \]"}
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $=\n\\left(\\begin{array}{ccc}\nc\_{11}  & c\_{12} & \\ldots  & c\_{1p} \\\\\nc\_{21} & c\_{22}   & \\ldots  & c\_{2p} \\\\\n\\vdots &  \\vdots   & \\ddots  & \\vdots \\\\\nc\_{m1}  & c\_{m2} & \\ldots  & c\_{mp} \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"= matrix \[ c\_(11) , c\_(12) , ... , c\_(1p) ; c\_(21) , c\_(22) , ... , c\_(2p) ; ... , ... , diagonal continuation , ... ; c\_(m1) , c\_(m2) , ... , c\_(mp) \]"} 
%[text] where
%[text]{"align":"center"} $c\_{ij} = \\sum\_{k} a\_{ik}b\_{kj}${"altText":"c\_(ij) = sum over k of a\_(ik)b\_(kj)"}
%[text] If the matrices' inner dimensions do not match, the rows of $A${"altText":"A"} will not have the same length as the columns of $B${"altText":"B"}, and this sum will not be defined.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Matrix multiplication](text:image:5795)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Example of matrix multiplication. Each element in the product is created by taking the dot product of a row of A with a column of B.*
%[text] ![](text:image:8879) **Exercise**. Multiply the matrices $A${"altText":"A"} and $B${"altText":"B"}. You can adjust the sizes.
m = 2; %[control:slider:0bee]{"position":[5,6]}
n = 3;  %[control:slider:557e]{"position":[5,6]}
p = 2; %[control:slider:1d32]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:4e16]{"position":[1,2]}
ValidateMatrices % This helper ensures that the matrices are defined
A = sym(Ai(1:m,1:n));
displayFormula(["'A = '", "A"])
B = sym(Bi(1:n,1:p));
displayFormula(["'B = '", "B"])
ShowSolution3 = true; %[control:checkbox:6fb7]{"position":[17,21]}
if(ShowSolution3)
    AB = A*B;
    displayFormula(["'AB = '", "AB"])
end
%[text] ![](text:image:0cc9) **Reflect.** 
%[text] 1\. Consider the matrix $\\mathbf{C}${"altText":"C"}: 
C = sym(Ai(end-n+1:end,end-n+1:end));
displayFormula(["'C = '", "C"])
%[text]     Which of the products are defined?
%[text]         (a) $\\mathbf{AC}${"altText":"AC"}
%[text]         (b) $\\mathbf{CA}${"altText":"CA"}
%[text]         (c) $\\mathbf{BC}${"altText":"BC"}
%[text]         (d) $\\mathbf{CB}${"altText":"CB"}
%[text] 2\. Is matrix multiplication commutative (i.e., is $\\mathbf{AB}= \\mathbf{BA}${"altText":"AB= BA"})?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4380665D] ## Special matrices
%[text] Specify the size of the matrices using the slider or click **show special matrices**.
n = 4;  %[control:slider:7070]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:54af]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_H_DC047D9D] **Square matrix**
%[text] A *square* matrix has an equal number of rows and columns. For example, the $n \\times n${"altText":"n times n"} matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"}:
A = sym(Ai(1:n,1:n));
displayFormula(["'A = '", "A"])
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_H_7EA101AF] **Diagonal matrix**
%[text] A *diagonal* matrix is a square matrix which is zero everywhere besides the main diagonal.
DA = sym(diag(Ai(1:n,n)));
displayFormula(["'D = '", "DA"])
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_H_7EA101AF] **Identity matrix**
%[text] An *identity* matrix, typically denoted $\\mathbf{I}${"altText":"I"}, is a diagonal matrix with each diagonal element equal to 1.
I1 = sym(diag(ones(n,1))); 
displayFormula(["'I = '", "I1"])
%[text] The identity matrix is so named because matrices remain identical after being multiplied by $\\mathbf{I}${"altText":"I"}:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{I} = \\mathbf{A}${"altText":"AI = A"}.
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:0f3d) **Exercise**. Multiply the $m \\times n${"altText":"m times n"} matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} by the $n \\times n${"altText":"n times n"} identity $\\mathbf{I}${"altText":"I"}.
m = 3; %[control:slider:2460]{"position":[5,6]}
n = 4;  %[control:slider:9883]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:3074]{"position":[1,2]}
ValidateMatrices % This helper ensures that the matrices are defined
I1 = sym(diag(ones(n,1))); 
A = sym(Ai(1:m,1:n));
displayFormula(["'A = '", "A","'    I = '", "I1"])
ShowSolution3 = true; %[control:checkbox:68c1]{"position":[17,21]}
if(ShowSolution3)
    AI = A*I1;
    displayFormula(["'AI = '", "AI"])
end
%[text] ![](text:image:10da) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - Do matrices remain identical after being added to $\\mathbf{I}${"altText":"I"}, i.e.: $\\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{I} \\stackrel{?}{=} \\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A + I = (?) A"}?
%[text] - What is the result of the product $\\mathbf{I} \\mathbf{A}${"altText":"I A"}?     \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B647422F] ## Matrix operations in MATLAB
%[text] In MATLAB, you will more commonly define numeric matrices. However, for more generic computations, you can define symbolic matrices using the Symbolic Math Toolbox. The basic functions for numeric matrices also work for symbolic matrices.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3577788E] ### Numeric matrices
%[text] **1. Define numeric matrices.** In MATLAB, you define a numeric matrix by listing its elements. When doing so, use spaces ( ) or commas (,) to separate elements and semicolons (`;`) to separate rows.  For example, to construct the matrix
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{A} = \\left( \\matrix{ 3 &  5 \\cr 10 & 12} \\right)${"altText":"A = ( matrix \[ 3 , 5 ; 10 , 12\] )"}
%[text] in MATLAB, you can use:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] A = [3 5; 10 12]
%[text] ```
%[text] ![](text:image:2e43) **Task**. Define the matrix 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{C} = \\left( \\matrix{ 1 &  2 \\cr 3 & 4} \\right)${"altText":"C = ( matrix \[ 1 , 2 ; 3 , 4\] )"}
%[text] as a numeric matrix in the space provided below.
C = [];
C = [1 2; 3 4]   %[control:editfield:4ad1]{"position":[1,15]} %[control:button:60e2]{"position":[16,17]}
IsEqualCheck(C,[1 2; 3 4]);
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:8ec8) **Task**. Also define the matrix $\\mathbf{R}${"altText":"R"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{R} =\\mathrm{}\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\n4 & 2\\\\\n2 & 9\\\\\n1 & 3\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"R =matrix \[ 4 , 2; 2 , 9; 1 , 3 \]"}
R = [];
R = [4 2; 2 9; 1 3]     %[control:editfield:99ab]{"position":[1,20]} %[control:button:68fe]{"position":[23,24]}
IsEqualCheck(R,[4 2; 2 9; 1 3]);
%%
%[text] **2. Perform matrix operations.** You can perform basic operations in MATLAB using the standard arithmetic operators (+, -, \*). An additional matrix $\\mathbf{P}${"altText":"P"} is defined below.
P = [1 -2; 1 -1; 2 5]
%[text] ![](text:image:4cc3) **Task**. Compute the sum `R + P` using MATLAB commands.
RpP = [R + P]   %[control:editfield:28ff]{"position":[8,13]} %[control:button:55fc]{"position":[15,16]}
if ~isempty(RpP)
   IsEqualCheck(RpP,R+P);
else
   disp("This is the default value. You must enter an expression.") 
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3d7c) **Task**. Compute `C + R`.
C = [1 2; 3 4];
R = [4 2; 2 9; 1 3];
try
   C+R   %[control:editfield:9fef]{"position":[4,7]} %[control:button:0bdf]{"position":[8,9]}
catch ExpectedError
   disp("Error: " + ExpectedError.message) 
end
%[text] Why does the operation fail?
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:797e) **Task**. Compute the product $\\mathbf{RC}${"altText":"RC"}. Hint: use the multiplication operator (\*).
RC = [R*C]   %[control:editfield:204b]{"position":[7,10]} %[control:button:684d]{"position":[12,13]}
IsEqualCheck(RC,R*C);
%[text] ![](text:image:7cba) **Reflect.** Why wasn't $\\mathbf{CR}${"altText":"CR"} computed?
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:48ba) **Task**. Compute the element-wise product of `R` and `P` `(R⊙P)`.
%[text] ![](text:image:4901) **Pro-tip**: MATLAB also allows element-wise multiplication of matrices. Element-wise multiplication is defined using the .\* operator.
RP = [R.*P]   %[control:editfield:41f9]{"position":[7,11]} %[control:button:2490]{"position":[13,14]}
if ~isempty(RP)
    IsEqualCheck(RP,R.*P);
else
   disp("This is the default value. Please enter an expression to evaluate.")
end
%[text] ![](text:image:4616) **Reflect.** What is the condition for the size of the matrices for the element-wise product?
%%
%[text] **3. Special matrices.**
%[text] ![](text:image:65fc) **Task**. Define a $4 \\times 4${"altText":"4 times 4"} identity matrix using the [`eye`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/eye.html) function with the syntax: `eye(n)`.
I = [eye(4)]   %[control:editfield:701b]{"position":[6,12]} %[control:button:723c]{"position":[14,15]}
IsEqualCheck(eye(4),I);
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:0b6d) **Task**. Define the diagonal matrix 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathrm{D\\ =\\ }\\mathrm{}\\left(\\begin{array}{cccc}\n7 & 0 & 0 & 0\\\\\n0 & 1 & 0 & 0\\\\\n0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\\\\n0 & 0 & 0 & 1\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"D = matrix \[ 7 , 0 , 0 , 0; 0 , 1 , 0 , 0; 0 , 0 , 3 , 0; 0 , 0 , 0 , 1 \]"}
%[text] in MATLAB. You can create a $n \\times n${"altText":"n times n"} diagonal matrix using the [`diag`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/diag.html) function with the syntax: `diag(v)`, where `v` is an $n${"altText":"n"}-element vector.
%[text] First, define the vector which contains the elements that will be placed on the diagonal.
v = [[7 1 3 1]]   %[control:editfield:87ed]{"position":[6,15]} %[control:button:3e56]{"position":[17,18]}
IsEqualCheck(reshape(v,1,[]),[7 1 3 1]);
%%
%[text] Afterward, generate the matrix using a call to `diag`.
D = [diag(v)]   %[control:editfield:4527]{"position":[6,13]} %[control:button:3be1]{"position":[15,16]}
IsEqualCheck(D,diag(v));
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:6090) **Task**. Compute the product $\\mathbf{ID}${"altText":"ID"}.
ID = [I*D]   %[control:editfield:288e]{"position":[7,10]} %[control:button:174a]{"position":[12,13]}
IsEqualCheck(ID,I*D);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_20375C4A] ### Symbolic matrices
%[text] There are two basic ways to define symbolic matrices in MATLAB.
%[text] 1\. To define a generic $m \\times n${"altText":"m times n"} symbolic matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"}, you can use the [`syms`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/syms.html) function with the syntax:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] syms A [m n] % Replace m and n with integers
%[text] ```
%[text] 2\. To define a symbolic matrix with specified entries, you can first define symbolic variables, and then put them into a matrix. For example:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] syms x y s 
%[text] B = [y x; s 3]
%[text] ```
%[text] ![](text:image:5fe4) **Task**. Define the two matrices shown below using the syms command.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{H} = \n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\nH\_{1,1}  & H\_{1,2} \\\\\nH\_{2,1}  & H\_{2,2} \\\\\nH\_{3,1}  & H\_{3,2} \n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"H = matrix \[ H\_(1,1) , H\_(1,2) ; H\_(2,1) , H\_(2,2) ; H\_(3,1) , H\_(3,2) \]"} $\\mathbf{G} = \n\\left(\\begin{array}{cc}\na & 1\\\\\nb & -3\n\\end{array}\\right)${"altText":"G = matrix \[ a , 1; b , -3 \]"}
%[text] Define `H` using the generic syntax.
H = [];
G = [];
syms H [3 2]   %[control:editfield:77c5]{"position":[1,13]} %[control:button:75c2]{"position":[14,15]}
H
IsEqualCheck([3 2],size(H));
%%
%[text] Define `a` and `b` as symbolic variables.
syms a b   %[control:editfield:2686]{"position":[1,9]} %[control:button:96cf]{"position":[10,11]}
%%
%[text] Define `G`. Use the symbolic variables `a` and `b`.
G = [a 1; b -3]   %[control:editfield:491c]{"position":[1,16]} %[control:button:275a]{"position":[17,18]}
syms a b
IsEqualCheck(G,[a 1; b -3]);
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:5f2c) **Task**. Compute the product $\\mathbf{HG}${"altText":"HG"}.
HG = [H*G]   %[control:editfield:82c2]{"position":[7,10]} %[control:button:2140]{"position":[12,13]}
IsEqualCheck(HG,H*G);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_023BA799] ### Application: grayscale images
%[text] Grayscale images are typically matrices of 8-bit integers with values in the range \[0,255\]. Run this section to load and display two images in MATLAB. 
%[text] ![Warning sign](text:image:1b4f) The images `rhino_gs.png` and `giraffe_gs.png` must be in the MATLAB Path for this section to run.
G = double(imread("giraffe_gs.png"));
R = double(imread("rhino_gs.png"));
imshowpair(G,R,"montage")
  %[control:button:6fb5]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] Each image is stored as a matrix of numbers. The images are quite large, so displaying all the numeric values is impractical. Instead, you can display a small submatrix of pixel values by selecting a range using [array indexing](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/math/array-indexing.html). For example, to select a submatrix of rows 20-30 and columns 40-60 of image `G`, you would use:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] Gsub = G(20:30,40:60)
%[text] ```
%[text] ![](text:image:619e) **Task**. The rhino image is stored in `R`. Select the submatrix of `R` from rows 100 to 106 and columns 300 to 320.
Rsub = [R(100:106,300:320)];   %[control:editfield:4442]{"position":[9,27]} %[control:button:2413]{"position":[30,31]}
Rsub
IsEqualCheck(Rsub,R(100:106,300:320));
%%
%[text] Because these two images are square and the same size, you can apply standard matrix operations to them.
%[text] ![](text:image:9559) **Task**. Add the two image matrices `G` and `R`. What image will G+R show?
GpR = [G+R];   %[control:editfield:1d72]{"position":[8,11]} %[control:button:3946]{"position":[14,15]}
IsEqualCheck(GpR,G+R);
imshow(GpR,[])
title("G+R")
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:2b31) **Task**. Compute `255-R`. What image will `255-R` show? Recall that the pixel values for this 8-bit image are in the range of \[0,255\].
result = [255-R];   %[control:editfield:0b1b]{"position":[11,16]} %[control:button:87ce]{"position":[19,20]}
IsEqualCheck(result,255-R);
imshow(result,[])
title("255-R")
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:8202) **Task**. Multiply the image matrices `G` and `R`. What image will GR show?
GR= [G*R];   %[control:editfield:6cc7]{"position":[6,9]} %[control:button:93c7]{"position":[12,13]}
IsEqualCheck(GR,G*R);
imshow(GR,[])
title("GR")
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:4b21) **Task**. So, matrix multiplication doesn't exactly produce a meaningful result for grayscale images. A more meaningful multiplication might be [element-wise multiplication](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.times.html) (denoted ⊙). Compute the element-wise product of `G` and `R`. What image will `G⊙R` show?
GdR= [G.*R];   %[control:editfield:5c9d]{"position":[7,11]} %[control:button:678a]{"position":[14,15]}
IsEqualCheck(GdR,G.*R);
imshow(GdR,[])
title("G⊙R")
%[text] ![](text:image:76d8) **Reflect.** Standard matrix multiplication is more difficult to compute and understand than element-wise multiplication. Why isn't the element-wise product considered to be the standard?
%%
%[text] ## ![](text:image:6507)**Further exploration**
%[text] - You can learn more about matrix operations and other linear algebra topics in the online course [Introduction to Linear Algebra with MATLAB](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/introduction-to-linear-algebra-with-matlab/linalg).
